   },
   {
    "code": "no_sdrf",
    "severity": "warning",
    "message": "No SDRF was found for this dataset, so each run was given a synthetic sample of its own. There is no sample metadata: treat every sample as unannotated."
   },
   {
    "code": "unplaced_ptm_sites",
    "severity": "warning",
    "message": "Some modifications on peptides shared between proteins were not written to ptm_sites for one or more of those proteins, because the peptide could not be located in that protein's sequence and the producer's residue spans cannot be paired with its accessions. Of the (PSM, protein) pairs affected: 4623 named a protein that does not contain the stored peptide -- typically a PSM ambiguous between peptide sequences (level 4 or 5), where this protein carries a different candidate than the first, which is the one stored. Sites on the other proteins of the same PSMs are written. Counts are in bundle.json under protein_databases."
   },
   {
    "code": "unresolved_modifications",
    "severity": "warning",
    "message": "2 modification(s) could not be mapped to a UNIMOD accession or a mass, and are carried in ProForma as [Info:...] tags: Common Biological:Hydroxybutyrylation on K, Trypsin Digested:GG (Ubiquitination Site) on K. Their ptm_sites rows exist and carry modification_name, with modification null, so a query by name finds them and a query by UNIMOD accession will not."
   },
   {
    "code": "upstream_provenance_changed",
    "severity": "warning",
    "message": "The search recorded its upstream 'db_prepare' provenance (db/provenance.json) with sha256 61a5ddc7c43d6e64686241464086050a1bb5544878659194515f908d4d6cb2e2, and the file there now has 812ce696ba3f0ee2a1f79241b5e16e9267d6199f7598af77391178b825658963: it was overwritten after the search. It is left out of this bundle rather than read as this search's record. The search's own record of the db_prepare stage is gone unless the producer archived it."
   },
   {
    "code": "collapsed_duplicate_rows",
    "severity": "info",
    "message": "The producer wrote some rows more than once, identical in every column, and the copies were dropped so each identifier appears once. Nothing was lost: the kept row is byte-for-byte what the duplicates said. Rows that share an identifier and disagree anywhere are still refused rather than collapsed. protein_groups: 1 identifier(s), 1 row(s) dropped, e.g. PXD067622:A6NCW0;A8MUK1;C9J2P7;C9JJH3;C9JLJ4;C9JPN9;C9JVI0;D6R901;D6R9N7;D6RA61;D6RBQ6;D6RCP7;D6RJB6;Q0WX57;Q7RTZ2 written 2 times; quant_values: 24 identifier(s), 24 row(s) dropped, e.g. PXD067622:20240830_HF_LC3_MAA_RK_12032_CA_DMSO4:label_free:protein_group:PXD067622:A6NCW0;A8MUK1;C9J2P7;C9JJH3;C9JLJ4;C9JPN9;C9JVI0;D6R901;D6R9N7;D6RA61;D6RBQ6;D6RCP7;D6RJB6;Q0WX57;Q7RTZ2:QuantProject:DEF-PROT-SPC written 2 times. The full list is in bundle.json under collapsed_duplicates."
   },
